      "proof_uses": [],
      "has_proof": false,
      "statement_event": 39,
      "proof_event": null,
      "proof_chapter": null,
      "proof_leanok": false,
      "text": "The {\\bf communication complexity of a randomised NOF protocol $P$ for $F$ with error $\\epsilon$} is the smallest time $t$ such that $$\\mathbb P(x \\mid P \\text{ is not valid at time } t) \\le \\epsilon$$ or $\\infty$ if no such $t$ exists."
    },
    {
      "id": "def:rand-complex",
      "kind": "definition",
      "position": 27,
      "chapter": "4-randomised-eval",
      "source": "chapter/4-randomised-eval.tex",
      "line": 14,
      "title": "Randomised complexity of a function",
      "labels": [
        "def:rand-complex"
      ],
      "lean_decls": [],
      "leanok": false,
      "mathlibok": false,
      "statement_uses": [
        "def:rand-protocol-complex"
      ],
      "proof_uses": [],
      "has_proof": false,
      "statement_event": 40,
      "proof_event": null,
      "proof_chapter": null,
      "proof_leanok": false,
      "text": "The {\\bf randomised communication complexity of a function $F$ with error $\\epsilon$}, denoted $R_\\epsilon(F)$, is the minimum of the randomised communication complexity of $P$ when $P$ ranges over all randomised NOF protocols."
    },
    {
      "id": "def:rand-eq-test-protocol-eval",
      "kind": "definition",
      "position": 28,
      "chapter": "4-randomised-eval",
      "source": "chapter/4-randomised-eval.tex",
      "line": 23,
      "title": "The randomised equality testing protocol for $\\eval$",
      "labels": [
        "def:rand-eq-test-protocol-eval"
      ],
      "lean_decls": [],
      "leanok": false,
      "mathlibok": false,
      "statement_uses": [
        "def:protocol"
      ],
      "proof_uses": [],
      "has_proof": false,
      "statement_event": 41,
      "proof_event": null,
      "proof_chapter": null,
      "proof_leanok": false,
      "text": "The {\\bf randomised equality testing protocol for $\\eval$} has domain $\\Omega := (\\Bool^d)^{\\lceil\\log_2\\epsilon^{-1}\\rceil}$ with the uniform measure and is defined by making participant $i$ do ``Compute $$a_{i, k} = \\sum_{j \\ne i} \\omega_{j, k}x_j$$ and send the sum of the digits of $a_{i, t/d}$ mod $2$ at time $t$'' and ``Guess $1$ iff the sum of the digits of $\\omega_i x_i$ + what participant $i$ said is $0$ modulo $2$''."
    },
